  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential": [
      {
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Smooth single-phase charts with positive finite entropy derivative in the adopted nonrelativistic macroscopic thermodynamic framework; the quantities are state functions, not empirical thermometer readings.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      "## Definition\n\nFix a material constitution $c$ and its equilibrium state domain $D_c$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq D_c$ be a specified open, single-phase chart in physical SI coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, with $U$ evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame of [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. On $\\Omega$, use the finite $C^2$ entropy function $S_c$ and the strict positivity $S_{c,U}>0$ adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Here $C^2$ means that all coordinate partial derivatives through order two exist and are continuous ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). A coordinate partial such as $S_{c,U}$ is the ordinary derivative in that coordinate direction while every other physical coordinate is held fixed, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]].\n\nDefine the **absolute thermodynamic temperature**, **entropic pressure**, and **molar chemical potential** of species $j$ by\n\n$$T_c:=\\frac{1}{S_{c,U}},\\qquad p_c:=T_cS_{c,V},\\qquad \\mu_{c,j}:=-T_cS_{c,n_j}.$$\n\nThe entropy unit is $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, the energy $U$ is in joules, $V$ is in cubic metres, and $n_j$ is in moles ([[def-td-si-quantities]]). Thus $S_{c,U}$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, $T_c$ is finite and positive in kelvins, $S_{c,V}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$ and $p_c$ has units pascals, and $S_{c,n_j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,mol^{-1}}$ and $\\mu_{c,j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. If a microscopic model instead uses a dimensionless particle count $N_j=N_{\\!A}n_j$, the corresponding per-particle chemical potential is $\\mu_{c,j}/N_{\\!A}$ joules, with the exact SI Avogadro constant from [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nThese are scalar state functions of the stated rest-frame equilibrium chart. The pressure in this definition is a derivative-defined conjugate; identifying it with force per area requires a separate adopted mechanical coupling. Neither an empirical thermometer calibration nor any equation of state is supplied by this definition."
    ],
    "post-td-mechanical-conjugate-identification": [
      {
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Positive-temperature simple-compressible equilibrium charts coupled to a quasistatic, frictionless planar piston of constant area; other work coordinates and nonequilibrium force laws are excluded.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      "## Postulate\n\nFor a simple-compressible system whose only admitted mechanical equilibrium coordinate is volume, adopt the following piston coupling. The boundary is a planar piston with constant area $A>0$ in $\\mathrm{m^2}$ and outward displacement coordinate $\\ell$ in metres; increasing $\\ell$ increases the enclosed volume by $dV=A\\,d\\ell$. Define the positive mechanical pressure $p_{\\mathrm{mech}}$ as the outward normal force exerted by the system on the piston divided by $A$, so $p_{\\mathrm{mech}}$ is in pascals. When the system is in quasistatic frictionless mechanical equilibrium with this piston, adopt the identification\n\n$$p_{\\mathrm{mech}}=p_c,$$\n\nwhere $p_c$ is the entropic pressure of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. With $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ positive for work done on the system, the same coupling assigns the incremental mechanical work\n\n$$\\delta W_{\\mathrm{on}}=-p_{\\mathrm{mech}}A\\,d\\ell=-p_c\\,dV.$$\n\nThe sign agrees with [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]: compression has $dV<0$ and positive work on the system. This is an adopted, regime-limited physical assumption, not a deduction from the entropy derivative or from the first-law balance [[post-td-first-law-energy]]. It makes no claim for friction, nonquasistatic processes, changing piston area, shear, surface forces, fields, composition transfer, or other generalized coordinates."
    ],
    "pthm-td-local-energy-representation": [
      {
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Local single-phase, positive-temperature charts of the adopted macroscopic entropy representation; no global inverse or statement at a phase boundary is asserted.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      "## Statement\n\nLet $c$ be a material constitution, let $r$ be its finite number of named species, and let $(U_0,V_0,n_{0,1},\\ldots,n_{0,r})$ lie in an open single-phase chart $\\Omega$ of physical SI coordinate values, with $U$ in joules, $V>0$ in cubic metres, and each $n_j>0$ in moles ([[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], [[def-td-si-quantities]]). The chart is the image of an open subset of the dimensionless domain $D_c$ under the fixed diagonal unit conversion $(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n)\\mapsto(U,V,n)$. Write $S_c(U,V,n)$ for the entropy on $D_c$ composed with the inverse unit conversion; its physical-coordinate partials include those unit factors as specified in [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Assume this representative is $C^2$ and $S_{c,U}(U_0,V_0,n_0)>0$. Then there are open neighborhoods of $(S_c(U_0,V_0,n_0),V_0,n_0)$ and $(U_0,V_0,n_0)$ on which the equation\n\n$$S_c(U,V,n)=s$$\n\nhas a unique solution $U=\\mathcal U_c(s,V,n)$ of class $C^2$. On this local chart,\n\n$$\\mathcal U_{c,s}=T_c,\\qquad \\mathcal U_{c,V}=-p_c,\\qquad \\mathcal U_{c,n_j}=\\mu_{c,j},$$\n\nwith the intensive functions defined by [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. This is a local representation only: it asserts neither global injectivity or domain coverage nor differentiability at a nonsmooth phase boundary."
    ],
    "pthm-td-interior-exchange-equilibrium": [
      {
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Attained global equilibrium maxima for two independent separated systems in smooth single-phase positive-temperature charts, with only the specified energy, volume, and species exchanges permitted.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      "## Statement\n\nConsider two independent separated subsystems $A,B$ of fixed material constitutions, with states in domains $D_{c_A},D_{c_B}$ as in [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], and additive total energy, volume, and aligned mole amounts as in [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] using SI coordinates ([[def-td-si-quantities]]). Let $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}=S_A+S_B$ be their adopted total entropy, and let $\\mathcal F$ be the exact feasible endpoint set fixed by the totals, retained partitions, and permitted exchanges in [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. Suppose $x_*=(x_A^*,x_B^*)\\in\\mathcal F$ is a stable equilibrium attaining the global entropy maximum adopted by [[post-td-entropy-maximum]]. Assume both subsystems lie in open $C^2$ single-phase charts with finite positive temperatures, and assume every exchange direction named below is locally feasible for both signs at $x_*$. Then:\n\n- If energy exchange is permitted, $T_A=T_B$.\n- If volume exchange is permitted, then $p_A/T_A=p_B/T_B$; if energy exchange is also permitted, this gives $p_A=p_B$.\n- If species $j$ can be exchanged, then $\\mu_{A,j}/T_A=\\mu_{B,j}/T_B$; if energy exchange is also permitted, this gives $\\mu_{A,j}=\\mu_{B,j}$.\n\nThe pressure equality concerns the derivative-defined pressures of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. It is equality of mechanical piston pressures only when the coupling of [[post-td-mechanical-conjugate-identification]] applies. A retained volume or species constraint supplies no corresponding equality."
    ],
    "pthm-td-concave-stability": [
      {
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "C2 single-phase entropy charts and explicitly convex affine slices of permitted exchange coordinates; the radial-curvature conclusion applies only to the separately designated common-constitutive-law bulk cone with its adopted entropy transport and common reference conventions.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      "## Statement\n\nLet $C$ be an open convex region of dimensionless numerical coordinates for a compound of independent separated systems, and let $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}:C\\to\\mathbb R$ be the adopted $C^2$ concave total entropy on a smooth single-phase chart. Let the feasible set be the affine slice $F=C\\cap\\{x:Ax=b\\}$ for a fixed real linear map $A$ and vector $b$. For the following conclusions, assume the mathematical input that for differentiable concave $f$ on an open convex $C$, an interior point of $C\\cap\\{x:Ax=b\\}$ whose derivative vanishes on $\\ker A$ is a global feasible maximizer; strict concavity gives at most one maximizer; for $C^2$ concave $f$, the Hessian quadratic form is negative semidefinite. Under this input, if $x_*\\in F$ is interior relative to this slice and $DS_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x_*)$ vanishes on $\\ker A$, then $x_*$ is a global entropy maximum on $F$. If $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}$ is strictly concave on $F$, that maximum is unique. The Hessian conclusion does not by itself give strict stability.\n\nThe mathematical input is supplied by [[lem-tdmath-concave-affine-maximizer-and-hessian]] on the stated region and admissible affine segments.\n\nThere is a further conditional scaling conclusion. On a designated open single-phase bulk cone $C_c\\subseteq D_c$ with the common constitutive law adopted by [[post-td-common-bulk-constitutive-law]], the $C^2$ entropy Hessian has zero radial direction: $D^2S_c(x)[h,x]=0$ for every $x\\in C_c$ and every vector $h$. This branch adopts coordinate-preserving entropy transport between the distinct scaled-system tag and one fixed material-law chart, with common species, units and reference conventions. The null-direction conclusion is not asserted for the general tagged family from [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] and [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] alone."
    ],
    "def-td-response-functions": [
      {
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Finite response derivatives on smooth positive-temperature simple-compressible charts at fixed composition; no positivity or finite-response claim is made outside the specified charts.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      "## Definition\n\nFix a constitution $c$ and a smooth physical-coordinate chart in $D_c$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], with fixed amounts $n=(n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$ and $T,p$ as in [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. A **valid response chart** is an open neighborhood on which the relevant independent coordinates used below have a $C^1$ inverse to the physical $(U,V)$ coordinates; a $C^1$ diffeomorphism has a $C^1$ inverse on open neighborhoods by [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. The existence of such a chart is a hypothesis for a response derivative, not a consequence of this definition. The derivatives use the indicated natural variables and hold all other listed coordinates fixed.\n\nThe **constant-volume heat capacity** and **constant-pressure heat capacity** of the whole system are, where the derivatives exist and are finite,\n\n$$C_V:=\\left(\\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial T}\\right)_{V,n},\\qquad H:=U+pV,\\qquad C_p:=\\left(\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial T}\\right)_{p,n}.$$\n\nHere $H$ is the enthalpy in joules; both heat capacities are in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, not per mole. The **isothermal compressibility** and **isobaric thermal expansion coefficient** are\n\n$$\\kappa_T:=-\\frac{1}{V}\\left(\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial p}\\right)_{T,n},\\qquad \\alpha_p:=\\frac{1}{V}\\left(\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial T}\\right)_{p,n}.$$\n\nSince $V>0$ and the coordinates use SI units ([[def-td-si-quantities]]), $\\kappa_T$ has units $\\mathrm{Pa^{-1}}$ and $\\alpha_p$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$. This definition asserts no sign for any response, no identity among them, and no finite value at a critical or otherwise singular chart."
    ],
